<setup>
  <selectFromSequence name = "a" from = "2" to = "3" step = "1"/>
  <selectFromSequence name = "b" from = "0" to = "2" step = "1"/>
  <selectFromSequence name = "c" from = "2" to = "3" step = "1" exclude="$a"/>

  <selectFromSequence name = "k" from = "0.33" to = "0.99" step = "0.33"/>
  <selectFromSequence name = "s" from = "-1" to = "1" step = "2"/>

  <function name="fP">$s*$k*1/4*(x+$a)*x*(x-$c)</function>
  <derivative name="fPP">$fP</derivative>

  <point name="A" styleNumber="2">(0,$$fP(0))
    <constraints>
      <constrainTo>$fP</constrainTo>
    </constraints>
  </point>

  <point name="B" styleNumber="1" draggable="false">($POI1,$$fP($POI1))</point>
  <point name="C" styleNumber="1" draggable="false">($POI2,$$fP($POI2))</point>

  <mathList name="correctCrNum">-$a 0 $c</mathList>

  <number name="POI1"> 1/3 * (-sqrt($a^2 + $a*$c + $c^2) - $a + $c)</number>
  <number name="POI2"> 1/3 * (sqrt($a^2 + $a*$c + $c^2) - $a + $c)</number>

  <number name="midPOI">($POI1 + $POI2)/2</number>

  <mathList name="correctPOI">$POI1 $POI2</mathList>

</setup>

<p>
  Consider the function <m>y = f(x)</m> whose <em>derivative</em> is given by the following graph.  Note that you can drag the included red point along the graph and see its coordinates.
</p>
      <graph xmin="-4" xmax="4" ymin="-4" ymax="4" size="medium" grid="true" showNavigation="false" fixAxes="true">
        <shortDescription>
          The graph of the derivative f-prime of an unknown function f
        </shortDescription>
        <description>
          The graph of <m>y = f'(x)</m> is shown, which looks like a cubic polynomial.  The graph of <m>f'(x)</m> crosses the <m>x</m>-axis at three locations: <m>x = -$a, 0, $b</m>.  In addition, <m>f'(x)</m> has a horizontal tangent line at <m>($POI1,$$fP($POI1))</m> where the rest of the nearby graph lies below the tangent line; the graph also has a horizontal tangent line at <m>($POI2,$$fP($POI2))</m> where the rest of the nearby graph lies above the tangent line.
        </description>
        $fP
        $A
        <label fixLocation anchor="(-3-0.2,$$fP(-3)+$s*0.25)"><m>f'</m></label>
        $B
        <label fixLocation anchor="($POI1-0.2,$$fP($POI1)+$s*0.25)"><m>($POI1, $$fP($POI1))</m></label>
        $C
        <label fixLocation anchor="($POI2+0.2,$$fP($POI2)-$s*0.25)"><m>($POI2, $$fP($POI2))</m></label>
      </graph>

<p>
  Again, the graph above is <m>y = f'(x)</m>.  Two key points on the graph of <m>y = f'(x)</m> are also labeled.
</p>

<p>
  <ol marker="a.">
    <li>
      <p>
        Use the given graph of <m>y = f'(x)</m> to estimate the critical numbers of <m>f</m> and report your results below.  If there is more than one critical number, enter them as a list separated by commas.  If there are none, enter <em>none</em>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <answer unorderedCompare matchPartial allowedErrorInNumbers="0.1" allowedErrorIsAbsolute>
          <mathInput name="crNum" >
            <label>
              The critical numbers of <m>f</m> are <m>x=</m> 
            </label>
          </mathInput>
          <award>
            <when> $crNum=$correctCrNum</when></award>
          </answer>
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        Use the given graph of <m>y = f'(x)</m> to estimate the points of inflection of <m>f</m> and report your results below.  If there is more than one inflection point, enter them as a list separated by commas.  If there are none, enter <em>none</em>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <answer unorderedCompare matchPartial allowedErrorInNumbers="0.25" allowedErrorIsAbsolute>
          <mathInput name="POI">
            <label>
              The inflection points of <m>f</m> are <m>x=</m>  
            </label>
          </mathInput>
          <award>
            <when> $POI=$correctPOI</when>
          </award>
        </answer>.
      </p>

      <!--
      <p>
        <m>POI1 = </m><number >$POI1</number>, <m>POI2 = </m><number >$POI2</number>
      </p>
      -->

    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        On each of the following intervals, decide whether <m>f</m> is increasing, decreasing, or sometimes increasing and sometimes decreasing, plus identify an appropriate reason that behavior occurs.
      </p>

       <ul>
         <li>
           <p>
            On <m>(-\infty, -$a)</m>, <m>f</m> is 
              <choiceInput name="incdec1" inline>
                <choice>?</choice>
                <choice>decreasing</choice>
                <choice>increasing</choice>
                <choice credit="0">sometimes increasing, sometimes decreasing</choice>
              </choiceInput>
            because <m>f'(x)</m> is 
              <choiceInput name="incdec1reas" inline>
                <choice>?</choice>
                <choice>always negative</choice>
                <choice>always positive</choice>
                <choice credit="0">sometimes negative, sometimes positive</choice>
              </choiceInput>
            on <m>(-\infty, -$a)</m>. 
            <!-- first choice = $incdec1.selectedIndices, second choice = $incdec1reas.selectedIndices, <m>f'(-$a-1) = $$fP(-$a-1)</m> -->
          </p>
          <answer>
            <award>
              <when>
                ($incdec1.selectedIndices=2 and $incdec1reas.selectedIndices=2 and $$fP(-$a-1) < 0) or ($incdec1.selectedIndices=3 and $incdec1reas.selectedIndices=3 and $$fP(-$a-1) > 0)
              </when>
            </award>
          </answer>
         </li>
         <li>
           <p>
            On <m>(-$a,0)</m>, <m>f</m> is 
              <choiceInput name="incdec2" inline>
                <choice>?</choice>
                <choice>decreasing</choice>
                <choice>increasing</choice>
                <choice credit="0">sometimes increasing, sometimes decreasing</choice>
              </choiceInput>
            because <m>f'(x)</m> is 
              <choiceInput name="incdec2reas" inline>
                <choice>?</choice>
                <choice>always negative</choice>
                <choice>always positive</choice>
                <choice credit="0">sometimes negative, sometimes positive</choice>
              </choiceInput>
            on <m>(-$a,0)</m>. 
            <!-- first choice = $incdec1.selectedIndices, second choice = $incdec1reas.selectedIndices, <m>f'(-$a-1) = $$fP(-$a-1)</m> -->
          </p>
          <answer>
            <award>
              <when>
                ($incdec2.selectedIndices=2 and $incdec2reas.selectedIndices=2 and $$fP(-$a+0.5) < 0) or ($incdec2.selectedIndices=3 and $incdec2reas.selectedIndices=3 and $$fP(-$a+0.5) > 0)
              </when>
            </award>
          </answer>
         </li>
         <li>
           <p>
            On <m>(0,$c)</m>, <m>f</m> is 
              <choiceInput name="incdec3" inline>
                <choice>?</choice>
                <choice>decreasing</choice>
                <choice>increasing</choice>
                <choice credit="0">sometimes increasing, sometimes decreasing</choice>
              </choiceInput>
            because <m>f'(x)</m> is 
              <choiceInput name="incdec3reas" inline>
                <choice>?</choice>
                <choice>always negative</choice>
                <choice>always positive</choice>
                <choice credit="0">sometimes negative, sometimes positive</choice>
              </choiceInput>
            on <m>(0,$c)</m>. 
            <!-- first choice = $incdec1.selectedIndices, second choice = $incdec1reas.selectedIndices, <m>f'(-$a-1) = $$fP(-$a-1)</m> -->
          </p>
          <answer>
            <award>
              <when>
                ($incdec3.selectedIndices=2 and $incdec3reas.selectedIndices=2 and $$fP(0.5) < 0) or ($incdec3.selectedIndices=3 and $incdec3reas.selectedIndices=3 and $$fP(0.5) > 0)
              </when>
            </award>
          </answer>
         </li>
         <li>
           <p>
            On <m>($c, \infty)</m>, <m>f</m> is 
              <choiceInput name="incdec4" inline>
                <choice>?</choice>
                <choice>decreasing</choice>
                <choice>increasing</choice>
                <choice credit="0">sometimes increasing, sometimes decreasing</choice>
              </choiceInput>
            because <m>f'(x)</m> is 
              <choiceInput name="incdec4reas" inline>
                <choice>?</choice>
                <choice>always negative</choice>
                <choice>always positive</choice>
                <choice credit="0">sometimes negative, sometimes positive</choice>
              </choiceInput>
            on <m>($c, \infty)</m>. 
            <!-- first choice = $incdec1.selectedIndices, second choice = $incdec1reas.selectedIndices, <m>f'(-$a-1) = $$fP(-$a-1)</m> -->
          </p>
          <answer>
            <award>
              <when>
                ($incdec4.selectedIndices=2 and $incdec4reas.selectedIndices=2 and $$fP($c+1) < 0) or ($incdec4.selectedIndices=3 and $incdec4reas.selectedIndices=3 and $$fP($c+1) > 0)
              </when>
            </award>
          </answer>
         </li>       
       </ul>   
      
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        On each of the following intervals, decide whether <m>f</m> is concave up (CCU), concave down (CCD), linear, or sometimes CCUp and sometimes CCD.
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            On <m>(-\infty, $POI1)</m>, <m>f</m> is 
            <choiceInput name="CCUCCD1" inline>
              <choice>?</choice>
              <choice>CCD</choice>
              <choice>CCU</choice>
              <choice>linear</choice>
              <choice>sometimes CCU, sometimes CCD</choice>
            </choiceInput>
            because <m>f'(x)</m> is 
            <choiceInput name="CCUCCD1reas" inline>
              <choice>?</choice>
              <choice>always decreasing</choice>
              <choice>always increasing</choice>
              <choice>always constant</choice>
              <choice>sometimes decreasing and sometimes increasing</choice>
            </choiceInput> 
            on <m>(-\infty, $POI1)</m>.
          </p>
          <answer>
            <award>
              <when>
                ($CCUCCD1.selectedIndices=2 and $CCUCCD1reas.selectedIndices=2 and $$fPP($POI1-1) < 0) or ($CCUCCD1.selectedIndices=3 and $CCUCCD1reas.selectedIndices=3 and $$fPP($POI1-1) > 0)
              </when>
            </award>
          </answer>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            On <m>($POI1, $POI2)</m>, <m>f</m> is 
            <choiceInput name="CCUCCD2" inline>
              <choice>?</choice>
              <choice>CCD</choice>
              <choice>CCU</choice>
              <choice>linear</choice>
              <choice>sometimes CCU, sometimes CCD</choice>
            </choiceInput>
            because <m>f'(x)</m> is 
            <choiceInput name="CCUCCD2reas" inline>
              <choice>?</choice>
              <choice>always decreasing</choice>
              <choice>always increasing</choice>
              <choice>always constant</choice>
              <choice>sometimes decreasing and sometimes increasing</choice>
            </choiceInput> 
            on <m>($POI1, $POI2)</m>.
          </p>
          <answer>
            <award>
              <when>
                ($CCUCCD2.selectedIndices=2 and $CCUCCD2reas.selectedIndices=2 and $$fPP($midPOI) < 0) or ($CCUCCD2.selectedIndices=3 and $CCUCCD2reas.selectedIndices=3 and $$fPP($midPOI) > 0)
              </when>
            </award>
          </answer>
        </li>  
        <li>
          <p>
            On <m>($POI2, \infty)</m>, <m>f</m> is 
            <choiceInput name="CCUCCD3" inline>
              <choice>?</choice>
              <choice>CCD</choice>
              <choice>CCU</choice>
              <choice>linear</choice>
              <choice>sometimes CCU, sometimes CCD</choice>
            </choiceInput>
            because <m>f'(x)</m> is 
            <choiceInput name="CCUCCD3reas" inline>
              <choice>?</choice>
              <choice>always decreasing</choice>
              <choice>always increasing</choice>
              <choice>always constant</choice>
              <choice>sometimes decreasing and sometimes increasing</choice>
            </choiceInput> 
            on <m>($POI2, \infty)</m>.
          </p>
          <answer>
            <award>
              <when>
                ($CCUCCD3.selectedIndices=2 and $CCUCCD3reas.selectedIndices=2 and $$fPP($POI2+1) < 0) or ($CCUCCD3.selectedIndices=3 and $CCUCCD3reas.selectedIndices=3 and $$fPP($POI2+1) > 0)
              </when>
            </award>
          </answer>
        </li>        
      </ul>
      
    
    </li>
  </ol>
</p>
